      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Exercise: background subtraction run ENTIRELY IN LATENT SPACE (a VAE twin of exercise_background_subtraction; decodes only to draw the overlay).\n\nA fixed-camera pedestrian clip (LoadVideo -> Video Slice -> Get Video Components = an IMAGE batch).\nLATENT PLATE (computed once): the whole batch is VAE-ENCODED to a LATENT batch, then 'CV Temporal Reduce' takes the MEDIAN over time IN LATENT SPACE (movers occupy a pixel a minority of frames, so they average out). 'CV Array -> Latent' re-wraps the plate as a LATENT (the fixed reference); 'VAE Decode' also turns it back into a viewable 'learned background' and seeds the output batch.\nPER FRAME (Inspire foreach, one body): 'VAE Encode' turns frame k into a LATENT -> 'CV Background Model (Update)' scores it against the latent plate (mean = latent plate, learning_rate 0). init_variance/min_variance are SMALL (1.0 / 0.01) because latents are ~unit scale, not 0..255 - the uint8 defaults would flag nothing. The foreground is at LATENT resolution (H/8,W/8); 'Overlay Masks' auto-resizes it up to the frame (nearest), so no explicit x8 upscale node is needed. No morphology despeckle (unlike the pixel twin): the 1/8-scale latent resolution already removes single-pixel speckle, and a pixel-scale open would erode whole objects. Overlay -> APPEND to the running batch. 'ForeachListEnd' yields the segmented batch -> 'Preview Image'.\n\nWhy latent scoring works: a fixed plate makes each frame independent (one accumulator -> foreach), and 'Background Model (Update)' is pure per-pixel float math, so it runs unchanged on encoded frames - only the variance SCALE differs. The overlay is drawn on the pixel frame because that is what a human views."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
